  "query_preview": "../Thursday through the weekend/\n\nThursday morning sees the beginning of a stronger atmospheric\nriver move into the area. Some rather impressive dynamics look to\nbe associated with this storm, with strong initial warm air\nadvection, and relatively cold air advecting eastward. These\ningredients coupled with sea surface temperatures still around \nthe mid 50s means that the environment leans more towards the\nconvective. Furthermore, a strong jet aloft with plenty of\ndivergence behind the warm front sets the stage for meso vertices\nforming along the front. These cause a number of issues: timing, \nlocation, and intensity of rainfall and highest winds can shift \ndrastically. Individual model runs tend to struggle with these \nenvironments, with run to run consistency of both the GFS and EC \ninconclusive on individual features. Ensemble guidance provides a \nclue on the location of highest wind speeds and rainfall however. \nAt this point, the southern half of the panhandle has the highest \nlikelihood of longer duration, heavier rainfall. Looking towards\nthe weekend, there is roughly a 60-40 split of active weather \ncontinuing and a stronger low passing south respectively. If \nactive weather continues, then generally southerly winds will \naccompany mostly rain for the panhandle. For the low passing \nsouth, this would invite stronger, downsloping northerlies, which \nwould dry out the panhandle and bring cooler temperatures \novernight.",
